-- A STRING-sorted FIELD, which Blawx's ontology has nowhere to put.
--
-- Blawx declares an attribute by picking its value type from a dropdown, and
-- that dropdown is a closed list. Measured on the stock v1.6.22-alpha checkout
-- (`/Volumes/transcend/src/blawx-stock`, `origin/main` at `6a717b1`):
--
--   blawx-blocks.js:5376       the `new_attribute_declaration` type field is
--                              [["true / false","boolean"], ["number","number"],
--                               ["date","date"], ["time","time"],
--                               ["datetime","datetime"], ["duration","duration"],
--                               ['list','list']]
--   blawx-blocks.js:5577-5583  the list it is re-populated from at runtime is
--                              those same six datatypes concatenated with the
--                              declared categories, with "true / false" put back
--                              at the front
--   scasp_generator.js:927     the generator's attribute arm branches only on
--                              boolean vs not-boolean and otherwise passes the
--                              dropdown value straight through into
--                              blawx_attribute(Cat,Name,Type)
--
-- There is no string option to select and no per-type branch that would emit
-- one, so this is a hard ceiling in the target, not a gap in the exporter: a
-- string-typed field, parameter or result is refused.
--
-- What a user does about it. If the values are a fixed vocabulary — which is
-- what most legislative "strings" turn out to be — write an enum, and it lowers
-- to a category plus one object per constructor:
--
--     DECLARE PlayerName IS ONE OF Jane, Bob
--     DECLARE Player HAS
--         name IS A PlayerName
--
-- (Measured 2026-09-02: that emits `blawx_category(player_name).` and
-- `blawx_attribute(player,name,player_name).`. Do not call the enum `Name`:
-- `Name` and `name` both mangle to the atom `name`, and the injectivity check
-- refuses the module with a second, unrelated-looking error.)
--
-- If the string was carrying IDENTITY rather than a value — a party's name used
-- only to tell two parties apart — drop the field: a record already lowers to a
-- category, and Blawx compares objects by atom, so the identity is already
-- there without a name to hang it on.
--
-- What is NOT refused: string LITERALS in a rule body. They mangle to atoms
-- through the program-global string-atom table and stay usable for equality
-- (BLAWX-EXPORT-SPEC §4.7). It is the typed field that has nowhere to go.
--
-- Measured 2026-09-02 (spec §11 W2) with `l4 blawx` on this file:
--   in `name`: STRING-sorted field or argument (Blawx): `name` is
--   STRING-sorted, and Blawx's ontology has no string attribute type — …

§ `String-typed field`

DECLARE Sign IS ONE OF Rock, Paper, Scissors

DECLARE Player HAS
    name   IS A STRING
    throws IS A Sign

@export 1. A player throws rock.
GIVEN p IS A Player
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `throws rock` p IF p's throws EQUALS Rock
